      "message": "The `guard` I suggested on PS1 goes too far: it also catches `raise-continuable`, so a handler around the call can\u0027t return a value to the procedure, and the generator ends.\n\n```scheme\n(define g (make-coroutine-generator\n           (lambda (yield) (yield (list \u0027got (raise-continuable \u0027warning))) (yield 2))))\n(with-exception-handler (lambda (e) 42) (lambda () (g)))\n```\n\nPS1 gives `(got 42)` and then `2`. PS2 raises \"an exception handler returned from a non-continuable exception\", and the next `(g)` gives eof. This happens on the first call too, which PS1 got right.\n\nSuspend the procedure at the raise instead of unwinding it:\n\n- Inside the `guard`, wrap `(proc yield)` in a `with-exception-handler` whose handler saves its own continuation as `resume` and passes the condition out through `return`, tagged.\n- The generator raises it again with `raise-continuable`. If the handler returns a value, resume the procedure with it and treat what comes back as this call\u0027s result.\n- If a plain `raise` gets a value back from the handler, the procedure raises a secondary error, which the `guard` passes on as it does now. If the caller\u0027s handler escaped instead, end the generator on the next call.\n\nA version along these lines gives `(got 42)` then `2`, still gives `second` for the PS1 example, leaves the procedure\u0027s own `guard`s alone, and passes srfi-158-generators.scm. Add the `raise-continuable` case to the test.",
